ame>Wunderlich</surname><given-names>AP</given-names></name><name><surname>Blankenhorn</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>No hypofrontality, but absence of prefrontal lateralization comparing verbal and spatial working memory in schizophrenia</article-title><source>Schizophr Res</source><volume>61</volume><fpage>175</fpage><lpage>84</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12729869</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b93-ndt-3-87"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name><name><surname>Golaszewski</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Mottaghy</surname><given-names>FM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Brain activation patterns during a selective attention test&#x02014;a functional MRI study in healthy volunteers and patients with schizophrenia</article-title><source>Psychiatry Res</source><volume>123</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>15</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12738340</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b94-ndt-3-87"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wykes</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Brammer</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Mellers</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Effects on the brain of a psychological treatment: cognitive remediation therapy</article-title><source>Br J Psychiatry</source><volume>181</volume><fpage>144</fpage><lpage>52</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12151286</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b95-ndt-3-87"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yucel</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pantelis</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Stuart</surname><given-names>GW</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Anterior cingulate activation during Stroop task performance: a PET to MRI coregistration study of individual patients with schizophrenia</article-title><source>Am J Psychiatry</source><volume>159</volume><fpage>251</fpage><lpage>4</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11823267</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-ndt-3-87" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>This graph illustrates how schizophrenic subjects might show a relative task-related hypoactivation, while being hyperactive in absolute terms.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ndt-3-87f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-ndt-3-87" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Sites of hyperactivation and hypoactivation in schizophrenia and mean activation in controls during mnemonic tasks (n-back and encoding, recognition and retrieval tasks). Most experiments gave just one point of maximal activation in the ACC but a few gave more. All are listed in <xref ref-type="table" rid="t1-ndt-3-87">Tables 1</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="table" rid="t3-ndt-3-87">3</xref>. The points anterior to the dashed grey line lie in rCZa, those posterior to the line lie in rCZp.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ndt-3-87f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-ndt-3-87" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Sites of hyperactivation and hypoactivation in schizophrenia and mean activation in controls during executive tasks (the Stroop, Verbal Fluency, Go-NoGo and Continuous Performance tasks). Most experiments gave just one point of maximal activation in the ACC but a few gave more. All are listed in <xref ref-type="table" rid="t1-ndt-3-87">Tables 1</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="table" rid="t3-ndt-3-87">3</xref>. The points anterior to the dashed grey line lie in rCZa, those posterior to the line lie in rCZp.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ndt-3-87f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-ndt-3-87" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>A graph illustrating one possible relationship between ACC activity, task difficulty and schizophrenia. Adapted from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b57-ndt-3-87">Manoach&#x02019;s (2003)</xref> theory of DLPFC activation in schizophrenia.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ndt-3-87f4"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ndt-3-87" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Results of the verbal, picture, and spatial 2-back mnemonic tasks</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Task/Symptom</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">PET/fMRI</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mean duration of Sz, yrs (sd)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Medication</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sz Performance</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ACC</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ACC x, y, z</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">PFC L</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">PFC R</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b13-ndt-3-87">Callicott et al (2000)</xref></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">verbal 2-back</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">fMRI</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.0 (6.0)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">on</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">worse</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02191;</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;2,14,40</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02193;</td><td align="